WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:09.884
Erin did not want to put on the bright blue raincoat that the lady at the kiosk handed

2
00:00:09.924 --> 00:00:10.204
to her.

3
00:00:10.224 --> 00:00:12.505
"Why do I have to wear this?"

4
00:00:13.046 --> 00:00:13.586
she asked.

5
00:00:14.586 --> 00:00:19.949
Erin's brother, Colin, smiled and said, "You'll be sorry if you don't put it on, trust me!"

6
00:00:20.829 --> 00:00:25.171
They were in the lineup to board the Maid of the Mist, the boat that would take them

7
00:00:25.271 --> 00:00:27.172
on a tour beneath Niagara Falls.

8
00:00:28.356 --> 00:00:32.499
It was Erin's first time visiting the falls and she couldn't wait to see them.

9
00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:38.303
Erin could hear the roar of the water ahead and, as she peeked over her brother's shoulder, she

10
00:00:38.383 --> 00:00:38.824
saw them!

11
00:00:39.824 --> 00:00:41.846
The view of the falls was astounding.

12
00:00:42.646 --> 00:00:47.410
The water from the Niagara River was flowing fast, thundering over a high cliff.

13
00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:52.974
A giant cloud of mist rose from the base of the falls, past the top of the cliffs.

14
00:00:53.894 --> 00:00:56.156
Erin was both excited and scared.

15
00:00:56.968 --> 00:01:01.711
She joined the rest of the tourists and climbed aboard the Maid of the Mist, where she was

16
00:01:01.771 --> 00:01:03.712
greeted by a friendly tour operator.

17
00:01:04.913 --> 00:01:11.136
Niagara Falls was formed over ten thousand years ago, when the area was covered by thick ice.

18
00:01:11.956 --> 00:01:17.139
As the ice melted, a few large glaciers began moving north, creating deep gouges in the earth.

19
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:23.743
These gouges formed the Niagara Escarpment, which is a ridge of cliffs and hills that follows a broad

20
00:01:23.803 --> 00:01:28.626
curve from New York State, through the Canadian province of Ontario, to Wisconsin.

21
00:01:29.527 --> 00:01:33.830
The Great Lakes were formed at the same time and are the largest group of freshwater lakes in

22
00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:34.311
the world.

23
00:01:35.251 --> 00:01:40.775
The water from the lakes created the Niagara River, and when the enormous volume of water flowed over

24
00:01:40.816 --> 00:01:45.759
the cliffs of the Niagara Escarpment, it created the high waterfalls of Niagara Falls.

25
00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:51.784
The falls look different and are in a different location today than when they were first formed.

26
00:01:52.712 --> 00:01:57.654
The great force of the Niagara River erodes the rock of the riverbed and the cliffs, causing the

27
00:01:57.714 --> 00:02:00.375
falls to recede a little further upstream every year.

28
00:02:01.316 --> 00:02:06.378
Since their formation, the falls have retreated several kilometers upstream from the escarpment.

29
00:02:07.839 --> 00:02:15.282
The Niagara Falls are actually three different waterfalls: the American Falls, the Bridal Veil Falls, and the Horseshoe

30
00:02:15.322 --> 00:02:22.654
Falls. The largest and most spectacular are the Horseshoe Falls, named because the water has eroded them into

31
00:02:22.694 --> 00:02:27.439
a shape like the letter "U," which resembles the metal shoe nailed to a horse's hoof.

32
00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:32.864
The Niagara Falls are located on the border between the United States and Canada.

33
00:02:33.724 --> 00:02:38.229
The Horseshoe Falls are on the Canadian side, and the other two on the American side.

34
00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:44.694
Although Niagara Falls is not the highest waterfall in the world, it is one of the widest and

35
00:02:44.735 --> 00:02:45.996
certainly the most famous.

36
00:02:47.036 --> 00:02:50.177
The falls drop about 52 meters at their highest point.

37
00:02:50.897 --> 00:02:58.079
The Horseshoe Falls are 792 meters wide and the American Falls, 323 meters wide.

38
00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:05.682
During the summer months, when the Niagara River is at its peak, more then 2,650,000 liters of water

39
00:03:05.742 --> 00:03:07.482
pass over the cliffs every second.

40
00:03:08.342 --> 00:03:15.277
Victoria Falls in Africa and Angel Falls in South America may be higher, but the magnificence of Niagara

41
00:03:15.317 --> 00:03:17.799
Falls makes them one of the most beautiful in the world.

42
00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:24.304
The area around the falls was first populated by the Clovis people, soon after the falls were formed.

43
00:03:25.304 --> 00:03:30.328
These ancient ancestors of the Native people of North America hunted the woolly mammoths at the end of

44
00:03:30.348 --> 00:03:31.028
the Ice Age.

45
00:03:31.869 --> 00:03:37.132
Traveling north, they spread throughout the continent and built small huts around the Niagara River.

46
00:03:38.073 --> 00:03:42.016
One thousand years ago, the Iroquois people settled in the same lands.

47
00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:46.623
They built small villages and cultivated the fertile land around the falls.

48
00:03:47.603 --> 00:03:51.886
The falls were so important to the Iroquois that they often appear in their stories and legends.

49
00:03:52.987 --> 00:03:58.611
In one Iroquois legend, a beautiful young woman was in love with the powerful god of thunder, who

50
00:03:58.651 --> 00:04:00.912
lived in a cave behind the Horseshoe Falls.

51
00:04:01.973 --> 00:04:06.636
Rather than marry the man chosen by her father, she threw herself over the falls to be with

52
00:04:06.676 --> 00:04:07.137
her lover.

53
00:04:08.037 --> 00:04:11.940
The name "Niagara" derives from an Iroquois word meaning "the channel."

54
00:04:13.396 --> 00:04:17.320
The first European explorers arrived at the beginning of the seventeenth century.

55
00:04:18.281 --> 00:04:24.006
Louis Hennepin, a French missionary, was the first explorer to write about the falls and described them in

56
00:04:24.046 --> 00:04:27.229
his diary as "an incredible waterfall which has no equal."

57
00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:33.094
As more settlers began arriving from Europe, the fame of Niagara Falls spread.

58
00:04:33.895 --> 00:04:38.940
Napoleon's brother traveled from France to visit the falls with his bride in the early nineteenth century.

59
00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:43.482
By the middle of the century, tourism was the main industry in the area.

60
00:04:44.322 --> 00:04:50.585
Niagara Falls was considered one of nature's greatest marvels and attracted many adventurers looking to build their fame.

61
00:04:51.505 --> 00:04:57.908
In 1859, the French tightrope walker Charles Blondin crossed from one side to the other on a three-inch-thick

62
00:04:57.988 --> 00:05:00.969
rope stretched high above the gorge.

63
00:05:01.069 --> 00:05:06.992
In 1901, a 63-year-old school teacher named Annie Taylor traveled over the falls in a wooden barrel.

64
00:05:07.964 --> 00:05:12.166
She emerged practically unscathed, exclaiming,"Did I go over the falls yet?"

65
00:05:13.366 --> 00:05:19.409
Countless other daredevils attempted to travel over the falls in boats or barrels; swimmers tried to swim over

66
00:05:19.449 --> 00:05:21.690
the falls or to cross the gorge at their base.

67
00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:26.792
Most, however, didn't survive, and daredevil stunts were outlawed in 1912.

68
00:05:28.152 --> 00:05:31.934
Today, the falls are even more popular as a tourist destination.

69
00:05:32.774 --> 00:05:36.876
More than twelve million tourists from all over the world visit Niagara Falls each year.

70
00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:43.205
One of the most popular tourist excursions is a trip on the Maid of the Mist; these boat

71
00:05:43.265 --> 00:05:46.167
rides have been in operation since 1846.

72
00:05:47.088 --> 00:05:51.912
The boats travel up the river to the foot of the falls, where their passengers, wearing raincoats and

73
00:05:51.972 --> 00:05:55.735
drenched by the mist, can experience the splendor of the falls close up.

74
00:05:56.736 --> 00:06:01.119
Tourists can also take a cable car that crosses the gorge to see the falls from above.

75
00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:06.183
They can even walk through a tunnel in the cliff and see the wall of crashing water from

76
00:06:06.223 --> 00:06:13.626
behind. Over the years, famous people have continued to visit the falls, which have provided the setting for

77
00:06:13.706 --> 00:06:14.586
countless movies.

78
00:06:15.306 --> 00:06:20.567
Marilyn Monroe made them a popular honeymoon destination in her 1953 movie, Niagara.

79
00:06:21.448 --> 00:06:26.529
The falls are spectacular at all times of the day, but especially at night, when they are illuminated

80
00:06:26.589 --> 00:06:27.489
by colored lights.

81
00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:32.891
As their boat approached the foot of the falls, Erin could feel the mist falling on her like

82
00:06:32.951 --> 00:06:33.551
heavy rain.

83
00:06:34.451 --> 00:06:36.732
She was happy she was wearing the blue raincoat.

84
00:06:37.568 --> 00:06:41.490
The roaring water plummeted in front of her, and she could feel it like thunder.

85
00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:43.971
Her heart pounded with excitement.

86
00:06:44.871 --> 00:06:49.374
In seeing Niagara Falls, she truly felt the power and beauty of nature.
